<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ahmad Jalayir</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 1410</span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Sultan Ahmad Jalayir</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ahmad Jalayir was the Jalayirid sultan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabriz">Tabriz</a> from 1382 to 1410, a poet, calligrapher and patron of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> painting. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> drove him from Baghdad in 1393 and 1401, and he took refuge with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> before returning. After Timur's death he tried to recover Tabriz, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qara-yusuf">Qara Yusuf</a> of the Qara Qoyunlu defeated and killed him in 1410.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ahmad-jalayir.jpg" alt="Ahmad Jalayir" width="320" height="467" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Maʿrūf Baghdādī for Sultan Ahmad (ruled 1382 to 1410) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Princely_couple._Jung-i_Ma%CA%BFr%C5%ABf_Baghd%C4%81d%C4%AB,_Tehran_Golestan_Palace,_MS_no._2184,_p._337.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
